          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Asthma with an acute exacerbation: is that one code, or a code plus something else?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** The corpus I searched does not settle whether asthma with an acute exacerbation takes one code or a code plus another. It does explain the main rule that governs the choice.\n\n**What the coding guidance says:**\n- Asthma in ICD-10-CM is coded in category J45. Severity and exacerbation are two separate axes, so asthma questions often come down to two lookups rather than one. (*Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes*)\n- Acute exacerbation and status asthmaticus are alternatives. They are not stages on one scale. If the record documents status asthmaticus, the code says status asthmaticus and does not also say acute exacerbation. The coder does not make that choice; the documentation does. (*Coding asthma: J45*)\n- If the record does not state severity, asthma is coded to the unspecified branch. The coder may not infer severity from the treatment given, a peak-flow reading, or how the condition is described elsewhere in the chart. (*Coding asthma: J45*)\n- If the record also documents chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), that category asks for an additional code naming the type of asthma, so both appear. (*Coding COPD: J44*)\n\n**What I could not confirm:** The passages I retrieved do not list the exact J45 subcodes for an exacerbation, and they do not say whether an additional code is required for the exacerbation itself. I have not filled that gap from memory. The exact code and any add-on should be checked against the current ICD-10-CM tables and the Alphabetic Index. Code sets are updated each October 1, so check the version in force for the date of care. A trained medical coder or the clinician's record is the right source for the final selection.\n\n**A safety note:** An asthma attack that is getting worse, or that makes breathing hard or leaves a person unable to speak in full sentences, is an emergency. Call your local emergency number now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
